<h3>The Face-to-Face Ask: Word-for-Word Scripts That Convert</h3>

<p>Most contractors feel awkward asking for reviews. They worry about being pushy or desperate. The solution is a scripted approach that feels natural and puts the customer first. Here are the exact scripts that convert best in field tests.</p>

<p>For one-day repairs, use this script: "Mrs. Johnson, I am glad we got your water heater working again. I know you probably checked our reviews before calling us, and I want to make sure other homeowners in your situation can find us too. Would you mind taking 30 seconds to leave a quick review on Google? I can send you the link by text right now."</p>

<p>This script works because it acknowledges the customer's own behavior. They did check reviews before hiring you. Everyone does. By pointing this out, you create reciprocity. They benefited from past reviews, so they feel obligated to contribute.</p>

<p>For multi-day projects, use this variation: "Mr. Smith, your new roof is complete. I hope it exceeds what you were expecting. We have found that the best way for homeowners like yourself to help other families find quality contractors is by sharing their experience online. Would you be willing to leave us a review on Google? I will send you a link that takes less than a minute."</p>

<p>The key phrase here is "homeowners like yourself." It positions the customer as part of a community helping other community members. People are more motivated to help others than to help your business directly.</p>

<p>For customers who seem hesitant, add this line: "You do not need to write a novel. Even two sentences about your experience helps tremendously." This removes the pressure of writing a long review and increases conversion by 25-30%.</p>

<h3>The Text Message Follow-Up: Why SMS Beats Email for Contractors</h3>

<p>Email review requests get buried in inboxes. The average open rate for service industry emails is 18-22%. Text messages have a 98% open rate, with most read within three minutes. For contractors, SMS is the superior channel for review requests.</p>

<p>The key to effective SMS review requests is brevity and timing. Send the text within 15 minutes of completing the job or finishing the walkthrough. The experience is fresh, and the customer still has their phone in hand.</p>

<p>Here is the exact text message template that converts at 35-45%:</p>

<p>"Hi [First Name], this is [Your Name] from [Company]. Thanks for choosing us for your [service] today. If you have 30 seconds, would you mind leaving a quick review? It helps other homeowners find us. [Review Link] No pressure at all, and thanks again!"</p>

<p>Notice what this message does not do. It does not ask for a five-star review. It does not offer incentives. It does not use corporate language. It sounds like a real person sent it, because a real person did.</p>

<p>For customers who do not respond within 48 hours, send one follow-up text: "Hi [First Name], just following up on the review request I sent. If you have a moment, I would really appreciate your feedback. [Review Link] Either way, thank you for your business!" This second message converts an additional 8-12% of non-responders.</p>

<h3>The Review Link Strategy: Making It Impossible to Fail</h3>

<p>Every extra click between your request and the review form costs you reviews. If a customer has to search for your business, find the review section, and log in, you have lost 60% of potential reviewers before they start. Your job is to eliminate friction entirely.</p>

<p>Create a direct Google review link using Google's Place ID tool. This link takes customers straight to the review form with your business pre-selected. No searching. No navigation. One click, and they are writing.</p>

<p>Shorten the link using a branded URL shortener like Bitly or Rebrandly. A link like "yourcompany.com/review" looks professional and trustworthy. Avoid generic shorteners like bit.ly/abc123, which can look suspicious.</p>

<p>Include the link in multiple touchpoints: your text message, a follow-up email, your invoice, and a printed card handed to the customer. The more places they see it, the more likely they are to act.</p>

<p>Test your link monthly. Google occasionally changes review URLs, and a broken link silently kills your review generation. Set a calendar reminder to click your own review link on the first of every month.</p>

<h3>The Invoice and Receipt Method: Capturing Reviews at Payment</h3>

<p>The moment a customer pays is a peak satisfaction moment. They have received the service, approved the work, and are completing the transaction. This is an ideal time to request a review.</p>

<p>Add a review request to your invoice template. Place it prominently, not buried in fine print. Use language like: "Your feedback helps us grow and helps other homeowners make informed decisions. If you were satisfied with our service, please consider leaving a review." Include your shortened review link.</p>

<p>For digital invoices sent via email or text, add a clickable button labeled "Leave a Review" in a contrasting color. Make it impossible to miss.</p>

<p>For printed receipts, include a QR code that links directly to your review form. Customers scan the code with their phone and are instantly at the review page. QR codes convert at 15-25% when placed on receipts, according to field data from contractor businesses.</p>

<p>One HVAC company in Phoenix added QR codes to their printed work orders and saw a 40% increase in monthly reviews within 60 days. The total cost was $12 for QR code stickers. The return was dozens of new reviews.</p>

<h2>Medium-Impact Strategies That Build Sustainable Review Flows</h2>

<p>Once you have implemented the high-impact strategies, these medium-impact approaches create ongoing review generation without daily effort. They require more setup but run largely on autopilot once established.</p>

<h3>Email Drip Campaigns for Review Generation</h3>

<p>Not every customer responds to your initial text or face-to-face ask. An automated email drip campaign catches the customers who need more time or prefer email over SMS.</p>

<p>Build a three-email sequence triggered 24 hours after job completion. The first email thanks the customer and includes the review link. The second email, sent 72 hours later, shares a brief customer story and asks again. The third email, sent one week later, makes a final soft ask.</p>

<p>Email 1 (24 hours): Subject: "Thanks for choosing [Company] - Quick favor?" Body: "Hi [First Name], I wanted to personally thank you for trusting us with your [service] yesterday. Your project turned out great, and I hope you are as happy with it as we are. If you have 30 seconds, would you mind sharing your experience in a quick Google review? [Link] Your feedback helps other homeowners find reliable contractors. Thanks again for your business. [Your Name]"</p>

<p>Email 2 (72 hours): Subject: "How is everything working?" Body: "Hi [First Name], I hope your [service] is working perfectly. We recently heard from another homeowner in [City] who found us through our Google reviews and was dealing with the same issue you had. Reviews from customers like you help families find quality help when they need it most. If you have a moment, here is that link again: [Link] Thank you for being a valued customer. [Your Name]"</p>

<p>Email 3 (7 days): Subject: "One last thing" Body: "Hi [First Name], I promise this is the last time I will ask. If you have not had a chance yet, a quick review on Google would mean a lot to our small business. [Link] Either way, thank you for choosing us. We are here if you ever need anything. [Your Name]"</p>

<p>This sequence converts an additional 12-18% of customers who did not respond to the initial SMS request. The key is the progression from direct ask to social proof to soft close. Each email has a different psychological trigger.</p>

<h3>Review Generation Cards and Print Materials</h3>

<p>Physical materials create tangible reminders that digital messages cannot match. A well-designed review card left with the customer converts at 20-30% when paired with a verbal request.</p>

<p>Design a simple 4x6 card with your logo, a thank you message, a QR code, and your shortened review link. The message should read: "Your feedback matters. Help other homeowners find trusted service by sharing your experience. Scan to leave a review in 30 seconds."</p>

<p>Hand the card to the customer during the face-to-face ask. Do not leave it on a counter or tape it to equipment. Physical handoff creates a personal connection that increases follow-through.</p>

<p>Some contractors include review cards in their follow-up mailings. A roofing company in Seattle sends a handwritten thank-you note one week after project completion with a review card enclosed. This personal touch converts at 35% because it feels genuine, not automated.</p>

<p>Consider adding review requests to your vehicle magnets, yard signs, and door hangers. A simple line like "Love our work? Review us on Google" with a QR code turns every job site into a review generation opportunity. One landscaping company added QR codes to their yard signs and received 15 reviews in a month from neighbors who saw the work in progress.</p>

<h3>The Team Incentive Program: Motivating Technicians to Ask</h3>

<p>Your technicians are your frontline review generators. If they do not ask, you do not get reviews. The problem is most technicians feel uncomfortable asking or simply forget. A structured incentive program solves both issues.</p>

<p>Start with training. Role-play the face-to-face ask during team meetings. Have technicians practice on each other until the script feels natural. Most discomfort comes from lack of practice, not genuine reluctance.</p>

<p>Create a simple tracking system. When a customer leaves a review and mentions a technician by name, that technician earns a reward. The reward does not need to be large. A $10 gift card, an extra paid hour, or public recognition in the company newsletter all work.</p>

<p>One electrical contractor in Dallas implemented a $5 bonus per named review. Their monthly review count increased from 4 to 23 within three months. The total monthly cost was under $100. The value of those reviews in new customer acquisition was thousands of dollars.</p>

<p>Run a monthly contest for most reviews generated. The winner gets a premium reward like a half-day off or a $50 gift card. Competition drives behavior, especially among field teams.</p>

<p>Track conversion rates by technician. Some will naturally excel at asking. Study what they do differently and share those techniques with the rest of the team. One technician might have a particularly effective phrase. Another might time the ask perfectly. These insights are gold.</p>

<h3>Automated Review Request Software for Contractors</h3>

<p>Manual review requests do not scale. Once you are handling 50+ jobs per month, automation becomes essential. Several tools are built specifically for home service businesses.</p>

<p>Podium integrates with most field service management software and automatically sends review requests via text after job completion. It also aggregates reviews from multiple platforms into a single dashboard. Pricing starts around $300 per month for small businesses.</p>

<p>BirdEye offers similar automation with additional reputation monitoring features. It alerts you to new reviews across platforms and provides response templates. Pricing is comparable to Podium.</p>

<p>LocalServiceHero is a newer option designed specifically for contractors. It connects to common contractor software like ServiceTitan and Housecall Pro. It costs less than the enterprise tools but has fewer features.</p>

<p>For budget-conscious contractors, Zapier connects Google Forms, your CRM, and review links to create basic automation. A simple Zap can trigger a text message when a job status changes to "complete" in your spreadsheet or CRM. The cost is $20-50 per month depending on volume.</p>

<p>Before choosing a tool, map your current workflow. The best software fits your process, not the other way around. If your team lives in ServiceTitan, choose a tool that integrates directly. If you run everything from spreadsheets, Zapier might be your best bet.</p>

<h3>Social Media Integration for Review Amplification</h3>

<p>Reviews do not live only on Google. Sharing positive reviews on social media amplifies their impact and reminds other customers to leave their own.</p>

<p>Create a simple graphic template in Canva featuring your best reviews. Post one per week on Facebook and Instagram. Tag the customer if they are comfortable with it. This public recognition encourages others to leave reviews in hopes of being featured.</p>

<p>When a customer leaves a particularly detailed or emotional review, create a short video response. Film yourself reading the review and thanking the customer by name. Post it to your Facebook page and Instagram Stories. This humanizes your business and shows you value customer feedback.</p>

<p>Add a "Reviews" highlight to your Instagram profile. Save your best reviews there so potential customers see them immediately when visiting your profile. One pest control company in Miami saw a 22% increase in profile visits converting to website clicks after adding a reviews highlight.</p>

<p>Include review links in your social media bios and pinned posts. Make it easy for followers who already know and trust you to leave reviews without searching.</p>

<h2>Advanced Review Generation Strategies for Scaling Contractors</h2>

<p>These strategies require more sophistication but deliver outsized returns for established contractors handling 100+ jobs monthly. Implement these after mastering the high and medium-impact approaches.</p>

<h3>The Review Funnel: Segmenting Happy and Unhappy Customers</h3>

<p>Not every customer should be asked to leave a public review. A customer who experienced a problem, even if you resolved it, might leave three stars instead of five. The review funnel solves this by directing happy customers to Google and unhappy customers to private feedback.</p>

<p>Build a simple two-step survey. Step one asks: "How would you rate your experience with [Company]?" with options for "Excellent," "Good," "Okay," "Poor," and "Terrible." Customers selecting "Excellent" or "Good" are directed to your Google review page. Customers selecting "Okay," "Poor," or "Terrible" are directed to a private feedback form.</p>

<p>This approach has two benefits. First, it filters potential negative reviews before they reach Google. Second, it gives you actionable feedback from dissatisfied customers that you can use to improve your service.</p>

<p>Implement the funnel using Typeform, Google Forms, or your review software. Send the survey link instead of the direct review link in your text and email requests. The extra click reduces total reviews by 10-15%, but the average rating of reviews you do receive increases significantly.</p>

<p>A plumbing company in Chicago implemented a review funnel and saw their average Google rating increase from 4.3 to 4.8 stars within six months. Their review volume dropped slightly from 18 to 15 per month, but the quality improvement more than offset the quantity decrease in terms of customer acquisition.</p>

<h3>Video Testimonial Conversion Strategy</h3>

<p>Video testimonials are more powerful than written reviews. They show real customers, real emotion, and real results. They also convert at 3-4x the rate of text reviews when used in marketing materials.</p>

<p>The barrier to video testimonials is customer reluctance. Most people do not want to be on camera. The solution is to make it effortless and low-pressure.</p>

<p>During your face-to-face ask, say this: "Mrs. Johnson, would you be willing to record a 30-second video about your experience? You can do it right here on my phone. Just say what problem you had, how we solved it, and whether you would recommend us."</p>

<p>Offering to record on your phone removes the technical barrier. Specifying 30 seconds removes the time barrier. Giving them a script removes the creativity barrier.</p>

<p>If they agree, ask three questions and edit the answers together: "What problem were you dealing with before calling us?" "How did our team solve it?" "Would you recommend us to a neighbor?" This structure creates a natural narrative arc.</p>

<p>Post video testimonials on your website, YouTube channel, Facebook page, and Google Business Profile. One HVAC company in Atlanta collected 40 video testimonials in a year and used them in their Facebook ad campaigns. Their cost per lead dropped 45% compared to ads without testimonials.</p>

<h3>Partnership and Referral Review Loops</h3>

<p>Strategic partnerships create review opportunities you cannot generate on your own. Partner with complementary businesses that serve the same customers but do not compete directly.</p>

<p>A roofing contractor can partner with a gutter company. When the gutter company finishes their work, they recommend the roofer and encourage reviews. When the roofer finishes, they return the favor. Both businesses gain access to pre-qualified customers who already trust the referring business.</p>

<p>Real estate agents are another powerful partner. Agents need reliable contractors for their clients and want to look helpful by making great recommendations. Build relationships with top agents in your market. Provide exceptional service to their referrals. Ask the agent to mention your reviews when making the introduction.</p>

<p>Home inspectors are similarly positioned. They interact with homeowners at the exact moment those homeowners need contractors. A home inspector who recommends your plumbing company and mentions your 200 five-star reviews sends you pre-sold leads.</p>

<p>Formalize partnerships with simple agreements. Each partner agrees to recommend the other and encourage reviews. Track referral sources so you know which partnerships deliver results. Double down on the ones that work and gracefully exit the ones that do not.</p>

<h3>Seasonal and Event-Based Review Campaigns</h3>

<p>Contractor businesses are often seasonal. HVAC companies are busy in summer and winter. Roofers are busy after storms. Landscapers are busy in spring and fall. Use these seasonal peaks to run concentrated review campaigns.</p>

<p>Before your busy season, prepare your team for an all-out review push. Set a specific goal, like 50 reviews in 60 days. Brief technicians on the importance of asking. Increase incentive payouts during the campaign period. Track daily progress on a whiteboard in the office.</p>

<p>After major weather events like hailstorms, hurricanes, or ice storms, homeowners are highly motivated to leave reviews. They are grateful for fast service during a crisis. One roofing company in Oklahoma City collected 80 reviews in the 30 days after a major hailstorm by having every crew member ask every customer.</p>

<p>Holidays create natural review opportunities too. Run a "Thank You" campaign in November where you reach out to all customers from the past year and ask for reviews. Frame it as helping other families find reliable service during the holiday season.</p>

<p>One plumbing company in Boston runs an annual "12 Days of Reviews" campaign in December. Each day, they feature a customer review on social media and enter that customer into a drawing for a $500 gift card. The campaign generates 40-60 reviews and massive social media engagement.</p>

<h3>Reputation Monitoring and Competitive Intelligence</h3>

<p>Advanced review generation includes knowing what competitors are doing and staying ahead of reputation threats. Reputation monitoring tools alert you to new reviews, brand mentions, and competitive moves.</p>

<p>Set up Google Alerts for your business name, your competitors' names, and key industry terms. Review these alerts weekly. When a competitor receives a negative review, study what went wrong. When they receive a positive review, note what the customer valued. This intelligence shapes your own service and review generation strategies.</p>

<p>Monitor review sites beyond Google. Yelp, Facebook, Angi, HomeAdvisor, and Better Business Bureau all matter for contractors. Some customers prefer specific platforms. A complete reputation strategy covers all of them, even if Google is your primary focus.</p>

<p>Respond to every review within 24 hours. Positive reviews get a thank you and a personal detail. Negative reviews get a professional acknowledgment and an offer to make it right. We will cover response strategies in detail in the next section.</p>

<h2>The Negative Review Playbook: Turning Critics Into Advocates</h2>

<p>Negative reviews are inevitable. Even the best contractors occasionally disappoint a customer. What separates good businesses from great ones is how they handle those negative reviews. This playbook gives you exact response templates and escalation procedures.</p>

<h3>How to Respond to Negative Reviews</h3>

<p>The goal of a negative review response is not to win the argument. It is to win the reader. Future customers who see your response should think, "This business cares and tries to make things right."</p>

<p>Follow this response structure for every negative review. First, apologize without making excuses. Second, acknowledge the specific issue. Third, explain what you are doing to prevent it from happening again. Fourth, invite the customer to contact you directly to resolve the issue.</p>

<p>Here is a template for a service quality complaint: "Thank you for your feedback, [Name]. I am sorry your experience did not meet the standards we hold ourselves to. We take your concerns about [specific issue] seriously, and I have personally reviewed what happened with our team. We have implemented [specific change] to ensure this does not happen again. Please call me directly at [phone] so I can make this right. [Your Name], Owner."</p>

<p>Notice what this response does not do. It does not argue. It does not blame the customer. It does not offer excuses. It takes responsibility, shows action, and invites resolution.</p>

<p>For pricing complaints, use this variation: "Thank you for sharing your experience, [Name]. I understand your concern about the final cost. We always provide written estimates before beginning work, and I would like to review your specific situation to see where the miscommunication occurred. Please call me at [phone] so we can discuss this directly. [Your Name], Owner."</p>

<p>The phrase "miscommunication" is intentional. It suggests a solvable problem rather than a fundamental disagreement. It opens the door to conversation without admitting fault on pricing.</p>

<h3>When and How to Remove Reviews</h3>

<p>Some negative reviews violate platform policies and can be removed. Google removes reviews that contain profanity, personal attacks, irrelevant content, or conflicts of interest. Yelp removes reviews from competitors or fake accounts.</p>

<p>Document every negative review immediately. Take a screenshot before it can be edited. Note the reviewer's name, date, and specific claims. This documentation supports removal requests and protects you if the situation escalates.</p>

<p>To request removal from Google, flag the review in your Google Business Profile dashboard. Select the reason that best applies. Provide a clear explanation. Google typically responds within 3-5 business days. Success rates vary, but reviews containing clear policy violations are removed 60-70% of the time.</p>

<p>For reviews that cannot be removed, your response is your only tool. Make it count. A thoughtful, professional response to an unfair review often impresses future customers more than the negative review itself.</p>

<h3>Turning Negative Reviews Into Positive Outcomes</h3>

<p>The best possible outcome from a negative review is converting that customer into an advocate. This happens more often than you might think when handled correctly.</p>

<p>When a customer leaves a negative review, call them within 24 hours. Do not email. A phone call shows you care enough to pick up the phone. Start by listening. Let them explain their full frustration without interrupting. Most people calm down simply by being heard.</p>

<p>After listening, apologize sincerely. Then ask, "What would it take to make this right?" This question puts the customer in control of the resolution. Often their request is reasonable and costs you little. One customer who complained about a messy job site asked for a free furnace filter. The filter cost $12. The revised review they left after the call generated three new customer inquiries.</p>

<p>After resolving the issue, ask if they would consider updating their review. Do not pressure them. Simply say, "I am glad we could work this out. If you feel our resolution was fair, an updated review would mean a lot to us. No pressure either way." About 30-40% of customers will update their review when asked politely after a positive resolution.</p>

<p>Track your negative review resolution rate. Set a goal to resolve 80% of negative reviews to the customer's satisfaction. Measure this monthly. A high resolution rate is a leading indicator of review rating improvement.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Review Generation for Contractors</h2>

<p>These are the most common questions contractors ask about review generation. The answers are based on real-world testing and platform policies.</p>

<h3>How many reviews do I need to rank in Google's local pack?</h3>

<p>There is no magic number, but competitive analysis provides guidance. In most markets, the top three local pack listings have 50-200 reviews. In less competitive rural markets, 20-30 reviews might be enough. In major metros like Los Angeles or Chicago, 100+ reviews are typically necessary.</p>

<p>Quantity matters, but velocity and rating matter too. A business with 50 reviews gained over six months often outranks a business with 200 reviews gained over five years. Google favors active, current businesses. A steady flow of 5-10 reviews per month signals ongoing customer satisfaction better than a static review count.</p>

<h3>Can I offer incentives for reviews?</h3>

<p>Google's policy prohibits offering incentives in exchange for reviews. This includes discounts, gift cards, entries into contests, or any other compensation. Violating this policy can result in review removal or account suspension.</p>

<p>However, you can offer incentives for feedback that is not platform-specific. For example, you can offer a discount on future service for completing a customer satisfaction survey. If the survey includes an optional review link, customers who had positive experiences may choose to leave a review. The key is that the incentive is tied to feedback, not to the review itself.</p>

<p>Some contractors run general referral programs that happen to generate reviews. "Refer a neighbor and get $50 off your next service" is allowed because the incentive is for the referral, not the review. Customers who refer often leave reviews too, but the review itself is not incentivized.</p>

<h3>How do I get reviews if my customers are not tech-savvy?</h3>

<p>Many contractor customers, particularly older homeowners, struggle with technology. The solution is to make the process as simple as possible and provide hands-on help when needed.</p>

<p>Instead of sending a link, walk the customer through the process on their device. Say, "Mrs. Johnson, would you mind if I show you how to leave a quick review? It takes 30 seconds." Open Google Maps, find your business, and hand them the phone at the review screen. This personal assistance converts at 70-80% with older customers.</p>

<p>For customers without smartphones, ask for a written testimonial on paper. Transcribe it and ask if you can post it to your website and Google Business Profile on their behalf. Most customers are happy to agree if you read the review back to them for approval.</p>

<h3>What should I do about fake negative reviews from competitors?</h3>

<p>Competitor fake reviews are frustrating but manageable. First, document everything. Screenshot the review, note the reviewer's profile, and check if they have reviewed other businesses in your industry. Patterns of competitor reviews are strong evidence for removal requests.</p>

<p>Flag the review to Google immediately. Select "Conflict of interest" as the reason. Provide evidence if available. Google takes competitor reviews seriously and removes them at a higher rate than general negative reviews.</p>

<p>Respond professionally regardless. Future customers do not know the review is fake. Your response should be: "We have no record of serving a customer by this name. We take all feedback seriously and would welcome the opportunity to discuss this directly. Please call us at [phone] so we can verify this experience." This response casts doubt on the review's legitimacy without making accusations.</p>

<h3>How often should I ask for reviews?</h3>

<p>Ask every satisfied customer once per completed job. Do not ask the same customer multiple times for the same job. Do not ask customers who expressed dissatisfaction during the service call.</p>

<p>For ongoing service customers, ask once per year. A customer who receives monthly lawn care does not need to leave 12 reviews. One annual review from a loyal customer is sufficient and more credible than multiple reviews from the same person.</p>

<h3>Should I respond to positive reviews?</h3>

<p>Yes. Responding to positive reviews shows appreciation and signals to Google that you are an active, engaged business. It also gives you an opportunity to include keywords naturally.</p>

<p>Keep responses brief and personal. Mention the specific service provided. Thank the customer by name. Sign with your name and title. Example: "Thank you so much, Mike! We are thrilled that your new water heater installation went smoothly. It was our pleasure serving you, and we are here if you ever need us. Sarah Johnson, Owner, Johnson Plumbing."</p>

<h3>Can I copy reviews from one platform to another?</h3>

<p>No. Reviews must be left organically on each platform. Copying a Yelp review to Google violates both platforms' policies and can result in review removal. Each platform wants unique content.</p>

<p>However, you can use review content in your marketing materials with customer permission. A testimonial from your website can be quoted in a Facebook post. A Google review can be featured in a brochure. Just do not repost the full review to another review platform.</p>

<h3>How do I handle a customer who threatens a bad review unless I give a refund?</h3>

<p>This is review extortion, and it violates Google's policies. Document the threat. Save emails, texts, or voicemails. Do not give in to demands made under threat, as this encourages future extortion.</p>

<p>Respond to any negative review by stating the facts professionally: "We are sorry you were dissatisfied. We attempted to resolve your concerns within our standard warranty terms. We do not believe a full refund was warranted based on the scope of work completed, but we remain open to discussing a fair resolution."</p>

<p>If the review contains threats or extortion language, flag it for removal immediately. Google removes reviews that include threats or demands.</p>

<h3>What is the best review platform for contractors?</h3>

<p>Google is the most important platform for contractors because it directly impacts local search rankings. Focus 80% of your review generation efforts on Google.</p>

<p>Facebook is secondary but valuable for social proof. Yelp matters in some markets but has strict filtering algorithms that hide many legitimate reviews. Angi and HomeAdvisor are relevant if you advertise on those platforms. Better Business Bureau reviews carry weight with older demographics.</p>

<p>Start with Google. Add Facebook once you have 50+ Google reviews. Consider other platforms based on where your specific customers spend time online.</p>

<h3>How long does it take to see results from review generation?</h3>

<p>Most contractors see measurable results within 30-60 days of implementing systematic review generation. Google typically indexes new reviews within 24-48 hours. Ranking improvements usually follow within 2-4 weeks of consistent review velocity.</p>

<p>Lead volume increases often lag ranking improvements by 30-60 days. Customers who see your improved listing today may not need your service for weeks. The compounding effect of reviews means results accelerate over time. A business generating 20 reviews per month sees faster growth than one generating 5 per month, even if both started at the same review count.</p>

<h3>Should I use a review generation service or do it myself?</h3>

<p>This depends on your time, technical ability, and budget. DIY review generation works well for businesses with 1-5 employees who can manage personal follow-ups. As you scale beyond 50 jobs per month, automation and services become cost-effective.</p>

<p>Professional review generation services typically cost $200-500 per month. They handle automation, monitoring, response writing, and strategy. For a contractor generating $500,000+ in annual revenue, this cost is negligible compared to the value of improved rankings and conversion rates.</p>

<p>The middle ground is using software like Podium or BirdEye and managing it internally. This requires 2-3 hours per week of staff time but costs less than full-service agencies.</p>

<h3>Can I delete my own negative reviews?</h3>

<p>No. Business owners cannot delete reviews from their Google Business Profile. Only the reviewer or Google can remove a review. If you believe a review violates Google's policies, you can flag it for removal, but the decision is Google's.</p>

<p>This is why the review funnel strategy is so important. Filtering dissatisfied customers to private feedback before they reach Google prevents negative reviews at the source. Prevention is your only reliable tool for controlling negative review volume.</p>

<h3>How do I get reviews for a new business with no customers yet?</h3>

<p>New contractors face the classic chicken-and-egg problem. You need reviews to get customers, but you need customers to get reviews. Break this cycle with these strategies.</p>

<p>First, complete a few jobs at cost or slightly above cost for friends, family, or community members. Treat these jobs exactly like paid work. Ask for reviews afterward. Five to ten initial reviews from real jobs give you a foundation.</p>

<p>Second, partner with local charities or community organizations. Offer free or discounted services in exchange for honest reviews. A roofing company that repairs a church roof and receives five reviews from board members starts with credible, detailed feedback.</p>

<p>Third, ask suppliers, subcontractors, and business partners for reviews if they have used your services. Google allows reviews from other businesses if they are genuine customers.</p>

<p>Fourth, focus on review quality over quantity initially. Three detailed five-star reviews are more valuable than ten generic one-sentence reviews. Encourage your first customers to write specific, detailed feedback.</p>

<h3>Do reviews on my website help SEO?</h3>

<p>Reviews on your website do not directly impact your Google Business Profile rankings, but they do help in other ways. Review content on your site provides fresh, keyword-rich text that search engines index. Customer reviews often contain natural language phrases that potential customers search for.</p>

<p>Schema markup for reviews can generate rich snippets in search results, increasing click-through rates. When your search result shows star ratings, you stand out from competitors who do not have them.</p>

<p>Website reviews also keep visitors on your site longer, reducing bounce rates. A customer who spends three minutes reading reviews is more likely to call than one who leaves after 30 seconds.</p>

<h3>What is review velocity and why does it matter?</h3>

<p>Review velocity is the rate at which you receive new reviews. Google measures this as part of its local ranking algorithm. A business that receives 10 reviews per month is seen as more active and relevant than one that received 50 reviews two years ago and none since.</p>

<p>Consistent velocity signals ongoing customer satisfaction. Sporadic bursts followed by silence suggest manipulation or business decline. Aim for steady review growth rather than sporadic spikes.</p>

<p>Track your monthly review velocity and set targets. If you complete 40 jobs per month and convert 25% to reviews, your velocity is 10 reviews per month. If your velocity drops, investigate why. Did your team stop asking? Did a technician change their approach? Velocity is a leading indicator of review health.</p>

